  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/geologiccontact/ontology">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"/>
    <dct:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2021-03-26</dct:created>
    <dct:creator rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/common/boyan_brodaric"/>
    <dct:creator rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/common/stephen_richard"/>
    <dct:creator xml:lang="en">CGI Concept Definition Task Group, Stephen Richard OWL adoption for Loop3D project</dct:creator>
    <dct:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode"/>
    <dct:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2020-01-29</dct:modified>
    <dct:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2021-03-26</dct:modified>
    <dct:publisher>ARC Loop3D project;  https://loop3d.org/</dct:publisher>
    <dct:publisher>Geological Survey of Canada, Natural Resources Canada, Government of Canada</dct:publisher>
    <schema:codeRepository>https://github.com/Loop3D/GKM</schema:codeRepository>
    <dct:rights>Copyright (c) 2021 Government of Canada</dct:rights>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">This vocabulary specifies Contact Type types, primarily taken from the IUGS Commission for Geoscience Information (CGI) Geoscience Terminology Working Group. Originally designed for use in the contactType property in GeoSciML Contact elements, adopt here as subClasses of gscn:Contact to harmonize with this ontology.</rdfs:comment>
    <dct:bibliographicCitation>Brodaric, B., and Richard, S.M., 2021, The GeoScience Ontology Reference: Geological Survey of Canada Open File 8796, 34 p., https://doi.org/10.4095/328296</dct:bibliographicCitation>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Geoscience Ontology, Contact</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Geologic contact types</skos:definition>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Geoscience Ontology, Contact</skos:prefLabel>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Metamorphic_Rock">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Composite_Genesis_Rock"/>
    <dct:source xml:lang="en">Jackson 1997</dct:source>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Robertson (1999, Classification of metamorphic rocks: British Geological Survey Research Report, RR 99–02) defines the boundary between diagenesis and metamorphism in sedimentary rocks as follows: “…the boundary between diagenesis and metamorphism is somewhat arbitrary and strongly dependent on the lithologies involved. For example changes take place in organic materials at lower temperatures than in rocks dominated by silicate minerals. In mudrocks, a white mica (illite) crystallinity value of less than 0.42 Delta 2 Theta obtained by X-ray diffraction analysis, is used to define the onset of metamorphism (Kisch, 1991). In this scheme, the first appearance of glaucophane, lawsonite, paragonite, prehnite, pumpellyite or stilpnomelane is taken to indicate the lower limit of metamorphism (Frey and Kisch, 1987; Bucher and Frey, 1994; Frey and Robinson, 1998). Most workers agree that such mineral growth starts at 150 +/- 50° C in silicate rocks. Many lithologies may show no change in mineralogy under these conditions and hence the recognition of the onset of metamorphism will vary with bulk composition.”</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Rock formed by solid-state mineralogical, chemical and/or structural changes to a pre-existing rock, in response to marked changes in temperature, pressure, shearing stress and chemical environment.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">metamorphic rock</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Porphyry">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Igneous_Rock"/>
    <dct:source xml:lang="en">LeMaitre et al. 2002</dct:source>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Igneous rock that contains conspicuous phenocrysts in a finer grained groundmass; groundmass itself may be phaneritic or fine-grained.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">porphyry</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
