  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Organic_Rich_Sediment">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Biogenic_Sediment"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/rockmaterial/Organic_Rich_Sedimentary_Material"/>
    <dct:source xml:lang="en">SLTTs 2004</dct:source>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Sediment with color, composition, texture and apparent density indicating greater than 50 percent organic content by weight on a moisture-free basis.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The broader relation from organic rich sediment to biogenic sediment is based on the inference that organic rich material is always biogenic in origin. Biogenic is a broader category because not all biogenic materials are organic rich, for example shells or phosphatic bone.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">organic rich sediment</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/geologicfoliation/joint_fabric">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Fabric defined by parting surfaces in rock that occur in crudely parallel orientation, spaced greater than 5 cm on average if in a very regular joint set. Spacing is more variable and alignment of surfaces is cruder that cleavage. Typically applies to geologic unit description.</rdfs:comment>
    <dct:source xml:lang="en">Borradaile et al 1982</dct:source>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">joint fabric</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/geologicstructure/Foliation"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/common/Role">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Roles are played by a relata in a relation, e.g. a person plays the role of employee in an employs relation.  The relata playing the role is the RolePlayer and the other relata are RoleObjects. Because a role (Mary as employer, John as employee) is derived from a specific relation, e.g.  Mary employs John, it mutully depends on a specific reified relation instance (Relator - the employment of John by Mary). Roles carry qualities -  indeed, they are are a bundle of qualities.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Role  </rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gso/1.0/common/Inherant"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd61eda891d324cf2b12b4208980eba67b158"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd61eda891d324cf2b12b4208980eba67b159"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="nd61eda891d324cf2b12b4208980eba67b160"/>
    <owl:disjointWith rdf:nodeID="nd61eda891d324cf2b12b4208980eba67b161"/>
  </rdf:Description>
